ry\",\n        \"M-peer\",\n        \"M-read-prefix-test\",\n        \"M-read-receiver-test\",\n        \"M-read-api\"\n      ],\n      \"pending\": [],\n      \"assessment\": \"unassessed\",\n      \"candidate\": true,\n      \"grounding\": {\n        \"source_ids\": [\n          \"M-read-queue\",\n          \"M-read-reply\",\n          \"M-follower-read\",\n          \"M-read-client\",\n          \"M-node-read-entry\",\n          \"M-peer\"\n        ],\n        \"expectation_ids\": [\n          \"M-read-prefix-test\",\n          \"M-read-receiver-test\",\n          \"M-read-api\"\n        ],\n        \"binding_ids\": [\n          \"BD-forwarded-context\"\n        ],\n        \"derivation\": \"M-read-prefix-test explicitly expects a prefix release to retain the individual ctx/from of each earlier status even when its index is advanced. M-read-receiver-test expects received Hint/HintHigh to become the readiness SystemCtx. M-read-api makes readiness completion belong to the returned operation, while M-read-client and M-node-read-entry implement that association with an exact SystemCtx batch key. Together these ground preservation of the original admitted context when routing a released status to its origin. They do not require immediate success for every admitted read or preservation of its old index. The suspect sender implementation is the subject of comparison, not the source of the expected output.\",\n        \"applicability\": \"Only actually exported readiness from qualified queued forwarded-read release; comparison retains coalesced index semantics and uses independent origin identity.\",\n        \"unresolved\": [],\n        \"conflicts\": [],\n        \"alternatives\": [\n          \"An implementation could legally coalesce read indices; that does not explain replacing the exact context key required by the consumer.\",\n          \"A permitted drop or timeout would not establish a context mismatch; missing result remains incomplete.\"\n        ]\n      },\n